  "body": "The current [definition](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#suffering-from-bad-input) of suffering from bad input:\r\n\r\n> Suffering from bad input\r\n> \r\n>     When a control has incomplete input and the user agent does not think the user ought to be able to submit the form in its current state.\r\n> \r\n>     When the setValidity() method sets badInput flag to true for a form-associated custom element.\r\n\r\nis too vague. It pretty much could be used to simply describe the general state of being invalid.\r\n\r\nI suggest:\r\n\r\n> Suffering from bad input\r\n> \r\n>     When a control has no value because the user interface describes input that the user agent cannot convert.\r\n> \r\n>     When the setValidity() method sets badInput flag to true for a form-associated custom element.\r\n\r\nP.S. With respect to saying \"no value\" instead of \"an incomplete value,\" I wrote it this way as I am unaware of a situation where bad input would not also mean value missing. It may perhaps be the case when there's a multiple attribute, but I am uncertain.",
